That would work provided that the two populations were fixed for opposite alleles at all of the markers considered.
I wouldn't normally include the F1 and parents among the data read into R/qtl, because they wouldn't be used for QTL analysis.

R/qtl doesn't include any particular facilities for inspecting the parents' genotypes. You can use pull.geno() to pull out all of the genotypes from the cross object as a big matrix (coded as 1=AA, 2=AB, 3=BB), with individuals as rows and markers as columns. And then you could look at the genotypes in particular rows, to see if the F0 parents are basically all AA or BB.

karl

